---
/**
 * `/app/about` — what this site is, and what it is not.
 *
 * The sentence this page is built around has been on every page since Session 8
 * ("A conceptual redesign of the Office of the Law Revision Counsel's site …
 * Not an official publication of the United States government") and it has been
 * in grey eight-point type below the fold the whole time. For anyone who lands
 * on a provision from a search engine, that is the most important sentence on
 * the screen and the least likely to be read.
 *
 * Three ways to fix it were on the table — an anchor from the navbar down to
 * the footer, a hover, and a page. A page won: the anchor scrolls someone to
 * two lines of small print and calls it an answer, a hover cannot be linked or
 * printed, and neither has room for the thing a reader actually needs next,
 * which is *how to check the text* — the release point it came from, the file
 * it was parsed out of, and how to see what changed.
 *
 * The footer keeps the sentence and now links here, so nothing moved out of
 * reach on the way.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { setPublicCache } from "../lib/cache";
import { APP, syntaxHref } from "../lib/url";

/** Prose about the project — it changes when the project does, not per request
 * and never per reader. Revalidate rather than pin: there is no release point
 * this could be immutable against. */
setPublicCache(Astro.response.headers, null, false);
---

<Base title="About · U.S. Code">
  <h1 class="doc-title">About this site</h1>

  <p class="lede">
    A conceptual redesign of the <a
      class="usa-link usa-link--external"
      rel="noopener"
      href="https://uscode.house.gov/">Office of the Law Revision Counsel</a
    >'s United States Code website, built entirely from the official USLM downloads.
    <strong>It is not an official publication of the United States government.</strong>
  </p>

  <div class="usa-summary-box" role="region" aria-labelledby="official-heading">
    <div class="usa-summary-box__body">
      <h2 class="usa-summary-box__heading" id="official-heading">
        Where to go for the official text
      </h2>
      <div class="usa-summary-box__text">
        <p>
          The Office of the Law Revision Counsel publishes the Code and is the authority
          on it. If you need the official text, or the currency of a title, go to{" "}
          <a
            class="usa-link usa-link--external"
            rel="noopener"
            href="https://uscode.house.gov/">uscode.house.gov</a
          >. Nothing here amends, replaces or interprets what they publish — every page
          on this site names the release point its text came from and links to the source
          file it was parsed out of.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 class="toc-group">What it is for</h2>

  <p>
    One question, asked well: <em>what did this provision say, at this moment?</em> The
    Code is republished in full at every release point, and a release point is named for
    the last public law it incorporates. That makes "the current text" easy to find and
    the text as it stood two Congresses ago surprisingly hard.
  </p>

  <p>
    So every URL here carries a release point, and the URL scheme mirrors the USLM
    <code>@identifier</code> the official XML already uses — <code
      >/us/usc/t16/s45f/c/5</code
    > is subsection (c)(5) of section 45f of title 16. Add <code>?release=</code> or{" "}
    <code>?date=</code> and you get that provision as it stood then. A citation you can
    write down by looking at it, and one that keeps answering.
  </p>

  <h2 class="toc-group">How to check anything on it</h2>

  <ul class="toc">
    <li>
      <a href={`${APP}/releases`}>
        <span class="toc__heading">
          Release points — every one this site holds, and which titles each changed
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={`${APP}/docs`}>
        <span class="toc__heading">
          The API — the same data as JSON, or as the source XML, verbatim
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href={syntaxHref()}>
        <span class="toc__heading">
          Search and citation syntax — what the one box in the header accepts
        </span>
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a
        class="usa-link usa-link--external"
        rel="noopener"
        href="https://uscode.house.gov/download/download.shtml">
        <span class="toc__heading">The official downloads this site is built from</span>
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Every section page links to its own version history, which lists each release point
    where its text changed, and to a redline between any two of them. That is the check
    worth knowing about: if this site has something wrong, the redline against the source
    XML is where it shows.
  </p>

  <h2 class="toc-group">How it is built</h2>

  <p>
    The corpus is the official USLM XML — 3,153 title-releases across 58 titles and 381
    release points — parsed and stored section by section. Text that a release point
    republished unchanged is stored once, which is what makes the whole history fit:
    about 91% of it deduplicates, because the Code republishes every title at every
    release point whether or not anything in it moved.
  </p>

  <p class="doc-meta">
    The source, the design decisions behind it and the build log are public:{" "}
    <a
      class="usa-link usa-link--external"
      rel="noopener"
      href="https://github.com/aih/uscode-redesign">github.com/aih/uscode-redesign</a
    >.
  </p>

  <p class="doc-meta">
    Some parts of the site are described here and not switched on yet — accounts and bulk
    downloads. The controls for those say so where they appear, rather than being hidden.
  </p>
</Base>
